final_answer_present": true, "final_answer_source": "json", "final_workspace": "/workspace/openbench-ab/results/step4-omp-sonnet-1017/omp-38/cells/with/trig-omp-bugfix-regress/7/workspace", "first_after_build": false, "first_side": "with", "fresh_subagents": 0, "harness": "omp", "harness_version": "omp/18.1.8 (/workspace/openbench-ab/results/omp-cache/bin/omp/1b5c7b53d2c460b2ede60d78591b6826facf0e4d/omp)", "harness_version_source": "host", "host": "grok-bot-vm-412615941", "image_digest": null, "last_activity_age_s": null, "list_has_generated": null, "main_agent_searched": false, "model": "claude-opus-5-5", "model_context_window": 1000000, "model_max_tokens": 128000, "output_tail": "`round_money(2.675)` now returns `2.68`.\n\nThe cause was Python's `round()`. It rounds half to even, and the float `2.675` is stored as `2.67499999\u2026`, so it came out as `2.67`. The fix in `pricing/money.py` converts the value through its shortest string form and rounds it with `Decimal` using `ROUND_HALF_UP`.\n\nI ran a few inputs. `round_money(2.675)` gives `2.68`, `round_money(1.005)` gives `1.01`, `round_money(2)` gives `2.0` and `add_vat(10)` gives `12.0`. The one existing test, in `tests/test_split.py`, still passes. I didn't add a test for `round_money`.\n\nNegative values round away from zero, so `round_money(-2.675)` gives `-2.68`. I assumed that is what you want for refunds and credits.\n\n`add_vat` multiplies in floating point before rounding. A product that lands a hair off a half-cent could still round the unexpected way. I didn't change that.", "paced_wait_s": 0.0, "params_stripped": [], "params_stripped_requests": 0, "permission_rejections": 0, "read_calls": 1, "replies_ok": 4, "replies_throttled": 0, "requests_cache_read": 102688, "requests_cache_write": 10204, "requests_cost_usd": 0.0469688, "requests_count": 4, "requests_input_uncached": 10, "requests_output": 1117, "reread": false, "rule_order": "none", "rule_prefix": "neither", "run_id": "omp:trig-omp-bugfix-regre